Hi all,

Below I attach a reply to a question (anonymized) that I have gotten several times now.  Short version: founder-event speciation/jump dispersal may or may not involve population genetic founder effects (severe population bottlenecks), and even if population-genetic founder effects are involved, there is no requirement that speciation must have taken place by the "founder-effect speciation", which is a very specific theory about how population genetic events might cause speciation.  BioGeoBEARS does not deal with these population-genetic questions at all. So, criticism of "+J" models on the argument that "founder-effect speciation" has not been supported in the literature, is fallacious.  If you have a reviewer or editor try this argument on you, don't let it slide!  The misconception seems to be somewhat widespread, despite my efforts to head off this misconception in Matzke (2014).

Full mini-essay posted below, or at http://phylo.wikidot.com/biogeobears-deals-with-founder-event-speciation-not-founder -- people are free to link to this and/or copy-paste, however they find it useful.

Cheers, Nick

